hifiberry-analog-recognition (0.4.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Detect analog playback from the analog input itself, not the shared output
    meter. The Playing/Stopped level was read from the output vu-meter (the 2x2
    sink monitor), which also carries whatever network player -- Spotify, Tidal
    Connect -- is running. On the analog-input device that meant starting a
    network stream raised the "analog" level, so the active-player logic
    switched to analog and stopped the stream that had just started, seconds
    in. The analog player now captures the analog node ("input-processor")
    directly via its own PipeWire stream and computes the level itself, so only
    real analog input is detected. The capture is targeted declaratively
    (target.object + stream.capture.sink); the session manager links it, no
    external pw-link.
  * Make the activation level selectable from the Web UI, in dBFS. The player
    descriptor gains a "threshold_dbfs" number/slider (range -80..-20 dBFS,
    default -50); the daemon re-reads it from the ConfigDB
    (player.analog-recognition.threshold_dbfs) on the settings poll and applies
    it live, no restart. [vu_meter] now takes threshold_dbfs + hysteresis_db
    instead of raw start/stop_threshold, and the level scale spans -80..0 dBFS.
    The deprecated [vu_meter] ws_url is ignored and warned about; old config
    files still parse. SettingsClient gains a 5s timeout so a wedged
    configurator cannot stall the state loop.
  * Add a GitHub Actions CI: fmt, clippy (-D warnings) and the unit/integration
    tests, plus a separate job that runs the PipeWire capture end to end
    against a headless PipeWire session.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 19:35:23 +0000

hifiberry-analog-recognition (0.3.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * postinst: stop writing the WebUI descriptor and icon into
    /etc/hifiberry/players.d. The package ships both under
    /usr/share/hifiberry/players.d, and configurator lets /etc shadow
    /usr/share, so the copy written here masked the packaged one and nothing
    ever read it. The two are byte-identical today, so nothing is visibly
    broken -- which is the reason to fix it now rather than later: the next
    field added to the shipped descriptor would be masked silently. That is
    how hifiberry-librespot lost a conflicts_with declaration and
    hifiberry-shairport lost its entire settings block, in both cases for
    weeks, with no error anywhere.
  * postinst: remove the stale /etc copy when upgrading from an affected
    version. A file present on a fresh install is left alone: there it is an
    administrator's own override.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 10:26:26 +0000

hifiberry-analog-recognition (0.3.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Notice when songrec is running but no longer recognising anything.
    songrec reaches PipeWire through pipewire-pulse. pipewire-pulse survives
    a restart of the PipeWire daemon and reconnects, but the capture stream
    does not come back -- and songrec neither notices nor exits. Its stdout
    never closes, so the supervision, which watches exactly that, waits for
    a line that can never arrive; Restart=on-failure never fires either,
    because nothing failed. Found on a device where songrec had been running
    for 20 hours holding no stream: the service looked healthy, the player
    reported "stopped", and analog recognition had simply been gone since
    PipeWire last restarted, four hours after boot.
  * The unit gains PartOf=pipewire.service, so a PipeWire restart restarts
    recognition with it. After= only orders startup and propagates nothing
    afterwards.
  * The service also checks for itself, for the cases systemd cannot see:
    every stream_check_secs (default 30) it confirms songrec still holds a
    capture stream, and restarts it when it does not. A check that cannot be
    run -- no pw-dump on the host -- counts as "still there", so a missing
    diagnostic tool cannot turn into a restart loop. stream_check_secs = 0
    disables it.
    Two consecutive empty checks are required before a restart, since pw-dump
    is an instantaneous snapshot a working songrec can be missing from, and
    pw-dump gets five seconds before the check is abandoned, so a wedged
    PipeWire cannot leave the watchdog silently not watching.
  * The version in Cargo.toml had drifted behind debian/changelog at 0.2.5;
    both now read 0.3.0.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Sun, 06 Sep 2026 07:23:01 +0000

hifiberry-analog-recognition (0.2.7) stable; urgency=medium

  * Ship the WebUI player descriptor and icon from
    /usr/share/hifiberry/players.d instead of /etc. A descriptor is package
    data, not configuration, and shipping it under /etc made it a conffile:
    on any device where the file already existed -- they were hand-placed
    before the packages shipped them -- dpkg treated it as a local edit and
    preserved it, silently, for ever. Such a device never received a single
    packaged update to its descriptor. The old conffile is removed on
    upgrade; /etc/hifiberry/players.d still works and takes precedence, for
    an administrator who genuinely wants to override or add a player.
    Requires hifiberry-configurator 2.17.6, which reads both directories.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 20 Aug 2026 20:00:00 +0000

hifiberry-analog-recognition (0.2.6) stable; urgency=medium

  * Publish as a HiFiBerry extension: declare the XB-Extension metadata so
    the package appears in the web interface's extension catalog. Removed
    from the hbos-full bundle, which now installs it on demand instead.
  * Ship the WebUI player descriptor and its icon
    (/etc/hifiberry/players.d/analog.json). These were never packaged --
    they existed only as hand-placed files on individual devices, so the
    player appeared in the WebUI there and nowhere else. As an extension
    that gap would be fatal: installing it would register no player at all.

